homIdealPolytope -- Compute the homogeneous ideal corresponding to the vertices of a lattice polytope in $\mathbb{R}^n$.
************************************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        homIdealPolytope W
  * Inputs:
      * W, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, list ${p_1,\ldots,p_r}$, where
        $p_1,\ldots,p_r$ are vertices of the lattice polytope in
        $\mathbb{R}^n$.
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note CoefficientRing:
        homIdealPolytope_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmCoefficientRing=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value QQ, choose the coefficient ring of the (output) ideal
      * *note VariableBaseName:
        multiReesIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVariableBaseName=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value "X", choose a base name for variables in the created ring
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, A homogeneous ideal of
        $k[x_1,\ldots,x_{n+1}].$

Description
===========

Given a list of vertices of a lattice polytope, the function outputs a
homogeneous ideal of $k[x_1,\ldots,x_{n+1}]$ such that the polytope is the
convex hull of the lattice points of the dehomogenization of a set of monomials
that generates the ideal in $k[x_1,\ldots,x_n]$.

The following example computes the homogeneous ideal corresponding to a 2-cross
polytope.

+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : I = homIdealPolytope {(0,1),(1,0),(0,-1),(-1,0)}|
|                                                     |
|             2       2     2     2                   |
|o1 = ideal (X X , X X , X X , X X )                  |
|             1 2   1 2   1 3   2 3                   |
|                                                     |
|o1 : Ideal of QQ[X ..X ]                             |
|                  1   3                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------+

The output can be used to compute the mixed volume of a collection of
polytopes. A list of the output ideals, corresponding to the vertices of
various polytopes, can be used as an input in the *note mMixedVolume:
mMixedVolume, function to compute the mixed volume of polytopes.

